      ],
      "orig": "1 Introduction",
      "text": "1 Introduction",
      "level": 1
    },
    {
      "self_ref": "#/texts/4",
      "parent": {
        "$ref": "#/body"
      },
      "children": [],
      "content_layer": "body",
      "label": "text",
      "prov": [
        {
          "page_no": 1,
          "bbox": {
            "l": 39.74999834375,
            "t": 605.0112637035767,
            "r": 295.0309481632699,
            "b": 525.0567490779175,
            "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"
          },
          "charspan": [
            0,
            412
          ]
        }
      ],
      "orig": "Text-to-speech systems for documents inherit whatever order the extractor produced. For single-column prose this is rarely a problem. For the two-column format used by  most conference  proceedings  it  is  catastrophic:  a  naive  top-tobottom  sweep  produces  a  sentence  from  the  left  column followed  by  an  unrelated  sentence  from  the  right,  and  the listener loses the thread within a paragraph.",
      "text": "Text-to-speech systems for documents inherit whatever order the extractor produced. For single-column prose this is rarely a problem. For the two-column format used by  most conference  proceedings  it  is  catastrophic:  a  naive  top-tobottom  sweep  produces  a  sentence  from  the  left  column followed  by  an  unrelated  sentence  from  the  right,  and  the listener loses the thread within a paragraph."
    },
    {
      "self_ref": "#/texts/5",
      "parent": {
        "$ref": "#/body"
      },
      "children": [],
      "content_layer": "body",
      "label": "text",
      "prov": [
        {
          "page_no": 1,
          "bbox": {
            "l": 39.74999834375,
            "t": 512.7612675473267,
            "r": 295.0309003746404,
            "b": 458.3067518591675,
            "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"
          },
          "charspan": [
            0,
            278
          ]
        }
      ],
      "orig": "The difficulty is that a page is a bag of positioned words. Nothing in the extracted stream says where a column begins or  ends.  Layout-analysis  models  can  recover  this  structure, but they are large, slow, and awkward to run on a personal machine alongside a speech model.",
      "text": "The difficulty is that a page is a bag of positioned words. Nothing in the extracted stream says where a column begins or  ends.  Layout-analysis  models  can  recover  this  structure, but they are large, slow, and awkward to run on a personal machine alongside a speech model."
    },
    {
      "self_ref": "#/texts/6",
      "parent": {
        "$ref": "#/body"
      },
      "children": [],
      "content_layer": "body",
      "label": "text",
      "prov": [
        {
          "page_no": 1,
          "bbox": {
            "l": 39.74999834375,
            "t": 446.01127032857664,
            "r": 295.0287468132703,
            "b": 366.80675567166753,
            "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"
          },
          "charspan": [
            0,
            401
          ]
        }
      ],
      "orig": "We  take  the  opposite  approach  and  ask  how  far  simple geometry  can  go.  Our  procedure  groups  words  into  visual rows, splits any row that spans a conspicuous horizontal gap, and then decides whether the page is genuinely two columns by  counting  how  many  independent  lines  fall  on  each  side. Pages that fail that test are read top to bottom, which is the less surprising failure.",
      "text": "We  take  the  opposite  approach  and  ask  how  far  simple geometry  can  go.  Our  procedure  groups  words  into  visual rows, splits any row that spans a conspicuous horizontal gap, and then decides whether the page is genuinely two columns by  counting  how  many  independent  lines  fall  on  each  side. Pages that fail that test are read top to bottom, which is the less surprising failure."
    },
